  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 958 PM MDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nDry slotting is beginning to intrude on the Four Corners at \nthis hour. This is acting to curb precipitation some across the \nregion. Light rain is still reaching the surface at many surface\nobservation sites. Some stronger rainfall is still coming down \nacross the San Juans. This is still an area of concern for \nrising river levels, as highlighted in the hydrological \ndiscussion below. Lower rain rates have kept flash flooding off \nthe table, but some basins could continue to rise due to the \npersistent nature of the rainfall. Some areas have already seen \nin excess of an inch of rainfall.\n\nOn Tuesday a pinched off low will drop out of the Great Basin\nmoving south of the Four Corners. This continues to pull dry air\nin behind it which is expected to suppress some of our rainfall\nin the northern half of the CWA on Tuesday. However, down south\nrich moisture wrapped into the low will continue to add to the\nflash flooding concern. Hi res models are keeping the stronger\nconvection south of the Four Corners on Tuesday, so rainfall\nrates could take a hit downward. \n\nThe low lifts out of the Rockies onto the Plains Wednesday, but\na strong wraparound moisture plume is expected to produce\nanother surge of rainfall for southern CO through the afternoon.\nSome colder air wrapped into the low looks to produce some\naccumulating snow on the San Juans and up the spine of the\nDivide Wednesday. Snow levels remain above pass level and much\nof it will melt out in the warmer afternoon temperatures. This \nshould be the final gasp of the tropical moisture, as rainfall \ntapers to a close by Thursday morning. High pressure begins to \nbuild into the region on Thursday. Temperatures return to near \nnormal then begin to climb through the weekend back into the 80s\nfor the desert valleys and into the 60s for the mountains.\nModels are playing with some showers cropping up on the\nmountains Sunday and Monday, but that remains a very tentative\nforecast at this point.",
